int/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157094 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{ 1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $X \\cap Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ consists of all the elements that are in both $X$ and $Y.$ To represent $X \\cap Y,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $X$ and $Y.$\n\n![](images/37/37243a3d1954c0ae7939c09ad05b6aa12a8dff8a3ae8915bbe2079051466cbcb.png)\n\nThe elements $1,$ $2,$ and $8$ are in both $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the intersection of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\{ 1,2,8 \\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157282 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a60a38ff-ea2f-5769-b039-ad633bf712bc" "question-a7438413-ff27-5848-88ce-6d1af7f5da3c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157086 :question/problem "Given the sets $X = \\{ 2, 5, 7, 8 \\},$ $Y = \\{ 1, 3, 5 \\},$ and $Z = \\{ 2, 4, 6, 8 \\},$ find $X \\cap Y,$ $Y \\cap Z,$ and $Z \\cap X.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $X$ and $Y.$ Since $X$ and $Y$ have only the element $5$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cap Y & =\\{2,5,7,8\\}\\cap \\{1,3,5\\} \\\\  & =\\{5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Y \\cap Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Y$ and $Z.$ Since $Y$ and $Z$ have no elements in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y\\cap Z & =\\{1,3,5\\}\\cap \\{2,4,6,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Z \\cap X$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Z$ and $X.$ Since $Z$ and $X$ have only the elements $2$ and $8$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z\\cap X & =\\{2,4,6,8\\}\\cap \\{2,5,7,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157283 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a242d62-5d06-5da0-a817-40f323401761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157087 :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $A \\cap B?$" :question/worked-solution "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{ 6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cap B$ is the *intersection* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & =\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,…\\}\\cap \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{12,24,36,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-adaf9cbf-33cd-56a7-8431-051b8d1058ce" :question/id #uuid "adaf9cbf-33cd-56a7-8431-051b8d1058ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-102094" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d4b1fa9cd4318ddba32a17ce858f4982cf7151bc5931b4163b2dc0ea6898e46.png)\n\nGiven the sets $A=\\{13,22,29,31,36\\}$ and $B=\\{29,31,36,42,47\\},$ find $A\\cap B.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $A\\cap B$ consists of all the elements that are in both $A$ and $B.$ To represent $A\\cap B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $A$ and $B.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c4523eaa405cb9f2140338beab8ed05e4409b7fafb2bf837ad8c22b985f5c56.png)\n\nThe elements $29,$ $31,$ and $36$ are in both $A$ and $B.$ Therefore, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{29,31,36\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0153a15-dd56-50b9-ab46-ddd368be6a1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0153a15-dd56-50b9-ab46-ddd368be6a1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0153a15-dd56-50b9-ab46-ddd368be6a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-fiel